<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Dhrangadhra State</h1><p class="st-meta">1742 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Dhrangadhra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The senior Jhala Rajput state of Kathiawar, which moved its capital from Halvad to Dhrangadhra in the 18th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhala">Jhala Rajputs</a> had ruled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhalawad">Jhalawad</a> region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> since medieval times, with their seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halvad">Halvad</a>. Around 1742 the ruler moved the capital to Dhrangadhra, giving the state its later name; <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wankaner-state">Wankaner</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/limbdi-state">Limbdi</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wadhwan-state">Wadhwan</a> were branches of the same clan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dhrangadhra was a 13-gun salute state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar-agency">Kathiawar Agency</a> ruled by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> Saheb. Its economy relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, salt from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-rann-of-kutch">Little Rann</a> and the local sandstone, still sold as Dhrangadhra stone. The rulers were reformers in education and administration in the early 20th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayurdhwajsinhji-meghrajji-iii">Mayurdhwajsinhji Meghrajji III</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and Dhrangadhra was merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saurashtra-state">United State of Saurashtra</a> in 1948. He later sat in the Indian parliament; the town is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surendranagar-district">Surendranagar district</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dhrangadhra-state.jpg" alt="Dhrangadhra State" width="280" height="234"><figcaption>Image: Lordo'Web · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dhrangadhra-Emblem-of-Raj-of-Dhrangadhra.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1742 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dhrangadhra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mayurdhwajsinhji Meghrajji III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dhrangadhra, Halvad-Dhrangadhra</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1845&amp;focus=dhrangadhra-state" aria-label="Dhrangadhra State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6589 1329 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dhrangadhra State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6909 1585L7364 1601M6909 1585L7364 1602M6909 1585L6896 1610"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6896 1610h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6909 1585h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6909 1585h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dhrangadhra. <a href="/atlas?year=1845&amp;focus=dhrangadhra-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
